use /lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Dismissal:
    description:
        Dismissal is how a popup, menu, or other temporary panel gets closed.
        The component that opens the panel is in charge of closing it, not the
        panel itself.

    triggers:
        A panel closes when the user presses Escape (or the platform's cancel
        key), clicks or taps outside it, or moves focus to something outside
        it. Any one of these is enough on its own. All three are on by
        default, but the component can turn any of them off, for example to
        stop a click outside from closing a panel with unsaved changes. How
        they are turned off is up to the chosen library.

    reporting:
        The component is told what closed the panel, so it can react
        differently. For example, it might keep what the user typed when focus
        moves away, but throw it away when they press Escape.

    focusReturn:
        When the panel closes, focus goes back to whatever opened it. The
        exception is when the panel closed because the user moved focus
        somewhere else on purpose; focus stays where they put it. If the thing
        that opened the panel is gone or can't take focus, the component picks
        somewhere else. Focus is never left pointing at nothing.

    ordering:
        When panels are open on top of each other, Escape closes only the top
        one. Clicking inside a lower panel closes everything above it and
        leaves that panel open. Clicking outside all of them closes them all.
        Whether that click also reaches whatever is underneath is up to the
        chosen library.

    nonGoals:
        - Scrolling does not close a panel. The panel moves with the thing it
            is attached to, so there is no reason to close it
